From 8e459f19e77aef4986e2b9b56535e8409cd2a264 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hellproof contributors <noreply@hellproof.invalid>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 17:52:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] leaf_prover: wrap an already-made Cairo proof
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`leaf_prover` today only has one entry point: give it a program and an
input, it runs the program, proves the run, verifies that proof inside
the cairo-verifier circuit and proves the circuit.

A caller that already holds the Cairo proof — it was produced on another
machine, by another front end (a wasm prover in a browser, say), or
simply earlier — has no way in, and has to re-run and re-prove the
program just to get back to the point where the interesting work starts.

Split the existing function at `prove_cairo`:

* `prove_leaf_from_proof(&CairoProof<Blake2sMerkleHasher>, &Program,
  &CircuitRegistry)` is everything after it. `prove_leaf` is now the run,
  the Cairo proof, and a call to it, so both paths build the same circuit
  and come out with the same circuit hash by construction.
* the binary grows `--cairo_proof <path> [--proof_format ...]`, which
  takes that path instead of running the program. `--program` stays: the
  program is what the circuit pins (it is baked in as constants and thus
  fixed by the registry's circuit hash), so it must come from the caller
  and not from the proof.

The output hash no longer comes from the runner. Both paths now read the
output cells from the proof's own public memory with the existing
`output_hash_from_output_cells` (made `pub`), which `verify_cairo_with_
component_set` already used for exactly this. That is not a weakening:
`CairoStatement::new` binds the digest to the proven public memory
through the public logup sum, so a proof and an output hash that do not
belong together make the circuit invalid.

Only the *extended* proof can be wrapped — the circuit is filled from
`ExtendedStarkProof::aux`, which `CairoProofForRustVerifier` drops — so
`--proof_format` names extended serializations only: `extended-binary`
(bincode, bzip2-wrapped or not, i.e. what `--proof-format extended-binary`
writes and what a raw `bincode::serialize(&CairoProof)` gives) and
`extended-json`. `ProofFormat::CairoSerde` is not offered: `CairoProof`
derives `CairoSerialize` but not `CairoDeserialize`, and the felt stream
carries neither `aux` nor `preprocessed_trace_variant`.

Two checks replace the ones the run path no longer implies: the proof's
`preprocessed_trace_variant` must be the registry's, and its column
counts must match the verifier config (the latter was already there).
